    "md5": "2cd4105c61b1595685c3c0555e0b6f9a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1853,
    "chunk_text": "As I went about brainstorming the modalities, including the timelines, I realised the research was going to be harder and that it was going to take longer than I thought. With patronage from the Martin Wickramasinghe Trust, I began work officially in November 2024 and went on until February the following year. Wrapping up preliminary research, including catalogues of Wickramasinghe\u2019s books and his newspaper articles, I commenced the second phase of the research with interviews. The first of these interviews was with Dr Sarath Amunugama; it was followed by conversations with 15 or so others who knew him, worked with him, or studied him. One of the most interesting of these interviews was with the ornithologist Professor Sarath Kotagama, who highlighted another aspect to Wickramasinghe which has been overlooked, his advocacy of simple science terms in Sinhala.\n\nBy now, the idea of two publications had transformed into one: a comprehensive study of Wickramasinghe. At first, I defined it as a coffee table book. When I suggested this to a renowned historian, however, the response was instantaneous.\n\nICES lecture, opening remarks by Ambassador Ravinatha Aryasinha and closing remarks by Priyanthi Fernando\n\n\u201cWhy is it only a coffee table book? Who wants a coffee table book on Wickramasinghe, it is ridiculous. Do a proper biography.\u201d\n\nBy then I had read two books which helped me reframe the structure of this work: Shanti Jayewardene\u2019s excellent study of Geoffrey Bawa, Geoffrey Manning Bawa: Decolonializing Architecture, and SinhaRaja Tammita-Delgoda\u2019s equally exceptional study of George Keyt, Absence of a Desired Image. They had been published seven years of each other, and I had been involved in the latter as research coordinator. Both books, and their authors, got me to think anew about the format and aims of my study."
  },
