    "chunk_size_bytes": 1887,
    "chunk_text": "Certain butterfly species, for instance, engage in mutually beneficial relationships with ants. The caterpillars produce sugary secretions that attract ants, which in turn provide protection from predators. However, this relationship comes at a cost.\n\n\u201cYou give us honey, and in return the ants get to eat some of your babies. It\u2019s a bizarre trade-off, but that\u2019s how the animal world operates,\u201d he explained.\n\nDragonflies, another group highlighted in the lecture, illustrate Sri Lanka\u2019s ecological significance. Of the 133 species recorded on the island, approximately 66 percent are endemic. Within this group lies the genus Ceylonosticta, or shadow damselflies, comprising 23 species found nowhere else on Earth.\n\n\u201cThese are incredibly special insects,\u201d he noted, emphasising their uniqueness.\n\nWijeyeratne also described one of nature\u2019s most astonishing phenomena \u2014 the migration of the globe skimmer dragonfly.\n\n\u201cThey start from India, come to Sri Lanka and the Maldives, lay eggs, and the next generation continues to Africa. It\u2019s an intergenerational migration \u2014 the longest known in insects,\u201d he said.\n\nA central theme of the lecture was accessibility. Sri Lanka\u2019s biodiversity is not confined to remote jungles but exists all around us \u2014 even within urban environments.\n\n\u201cYou don\u2019t have to go far from Colombo to see amazing endemic biodiversity \u2014 you just need to know where to look,\u201d Wijeyeratne said.\n\nHe recounted how dragonflies are sometimes deceived by modern urban landscapes.\n\n\u201cThe wandering skimmer will try to lay its eggs on car roofs because it mistakes the reflection for a water body,\u201d he said, drawing laughter from the audience.\n\nEven a simple rock pool, he explained, can reveal multiple branches of life.\n\n\u201cIf you really want to see biodiversity across major animal groups, just look into a rock pool,\u201d he added."
