    "chunk_size_bytes": 1946,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThe wandering skimmer will try to lay its eggs on car roofs because it mistakes the reflection for a water body,\u201d he said, drawing laughter from the audience.\n\nEven a simple rock pool, he explained, can reveal multiple branches of life.\n\n\u201cIf you really want to see biodiversity across major animal groups, just look into a rock pool,\u201d he added.\n\n\u201cIt\u2019s hard to imagine, but land snails, marine snails and squid all belong to the same phylum,\u201d he explained, illustrating the surprising connections within the tree of life.\n\nHe also discussed echinoderms \u2014 starfish and sea urchins \u2014 which can be found in coastal rock pools and represent another major branch of animal evolution.\n\n\u201cThese simple habitats can show you an incredible cross-section of life,\u201d he noted.\n\n\u201cWhat we call fish is actually a complicated grouping \u2014 it\u2019s not a simple evolutionary category,\u201d he explained.\n\n\u201cI am more closely related to a humphead wrasse than that fish is to a shark,\u201d highlighting the complexities of evolutionary relationships.\n\n\u201cWhen the dominant male dies, the female can change sex and take over. It\u2019s an amazing adaptation that ensures continuity,\u201d he said.\n\nFreshwater species, such as killifish, demonstrate equally fascinating resilience. Their eggs can survive in dry conditions and hatch when water returns.\n\n\u201cYou might even find fish appearing in a puddle in your garden after rain, because the eggs were lying dormant,\u201d he said.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s amphibians represent one of its greatest scientific success stories. Of the 107 recorded species, an extraordinary 88 percent are endemic.\n\nWijeyeratne described recent decades as a \u201crenaissance\u201d in biodiversity research, with scientists uncovering a wealth of previously unknown species.\n\n\u201cThis work has transformed our understanding of Sri Lanka\u2019s biodiversity,\u201d he said.\n\nThe lecture also challenged common assumptions about reptiles and birds."
