    "md5": "6b0f5b8a0a42d79b778cfe316cd3dcc2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1667,
    "chunk_text": "Reverend Father Aloysius Pieris \u2013 A Brave Priest Who Did Not Fear Controversy\n\nReverend Father Aloysius Pieris, perhaps the best-known Jesuit priest in Sri Lanka of the last century, passed away two days ago. He played many diverse roles within the Church and in the realm of social issues. At the time of his death, he was at the Tulana Research Center, an institution he played a prominent role in organizing. He passed away in the hospital after a prolonged illness, cared for deeply by former students of the Jesuits and many others who admired him.\n\nIt is too early to offer a lengthy assessment of the life, work, and times of Fr. Aloy Pieris. He was involved in so many things\u2014not merely activities, but also deep, in-depth studies and teaching. To gain even a glimpse into the life of this important thinker and actor, it is essential to point to a key turning point for those who knew him over a long period.\n\nI have known him since the 1970s, and it is clear he underwent a complete conversion to the task of promoting the teachings of the Second Vatican Council (Vatican II), which was held in the early 1960s. Before that, he had been in Rome, studying and teaching, and was fully aware of the debates taking place at the time, particularly in religious and Catholic circles.\n\nThe central theme of the Vatican II debates was to bring the Catholic Church to confront the problems faced by humanity in the 20th century. Life across the globe had changed drastically from earlier times, marked by industrialization in the West, secularization, and the emergence of new power blocs organized around capitalist and communist worldviews during the Cold War."
