    "chunk_size_bytes": 1917,
    "chunk_text": "Although this story requires a fuller telling, it is important to note that promoting Vatican II was not always a smooth affair. It brought bitter controversies and serious divisions within the Catholic Church. Older priests and leaders often found themselves in conflict with what they were now called to do. Sorting out these conflicts became a preoccupation for decades and, in some ways, continues to this day.\n\nOther prominent leaders emerged, including Fr. Tissa Balasuriya of the Centre for Society and Religion and Fr. Aloy Pieris, Director of the Tulana Research Center. Both were intellectuals of great merit with thorough knowledge of the Vatican II controversies, and they used their forums to promote its orientations in Sri Lanka. A substantial body of literature from this period remains, documenting what took place.\n\nIt was also during this time that a group of theologians, known as the Asian theologians, developed, with Fr. Balasuriya playing a prominent role and Fr. Aloy Pieris taking an active part.\n\nOne unfortunate incident during this time was a misunderstanding surrounding Fr. Tissa Balasuriya\u2019s book\u00a0Mary and Human Liberation. Written within orthodox Catholic teaching but emphasizing the role of women in the modern Church, the book was controversially portrayed as heretical, leading to a Vatican order of excommunication. The Oblate order, which had its own highly qualified theologians in Sri Lanka, declared the work orthodox. After interventions by globally known theologians in dialogue with Fr. Balasuriya, the matter was resolved and the excommunication withdrawn. Fr. Aloy Pieris, in close consultation with Fr. Balasuriya during that time, witnessed how this controversy left a deep bitterness in a man known for a lifetime of commitment to the Church, including roles as defender of the Church during the school takeover issue and as director of Aquinas University College."
